Reason:
The `ServerSocket` is being created without specifying a specific network interface, which causes it to bind to all available interfaces. This behavior leads to test failures when the tests expect that subsequent attempts to bind to the same port will fail, as the port is still considered open on other interfaces.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
InetAddress addr = InetAddress.getByName("localhost");
ServerSocket s = new ServerSocket(port, 50, addr);
```
```java
InetAddress addr = InetAddress.getByName("localhost");
ServerSocket s = new ServerSocket(port[0], 50, addr);
```
```java
InetAddress addr = InetAddress.getByName("localhost");
ServerSocket s = new ServerSocket(port[0], 50, addr);
```
Reason:
By specifying `InetAddress.getByName("localhost")` when creating the `ServerSocket`, the binding is restricted to the localhost interface only. This ensures that subsequent attempts to bind to the same port will fail as expected, aligning the behavior with the tests' assumptions about port availability and network configuration.

